              is a stratified map of towers that fiberwise preserves the framing. (In fact, this makes mesh maps framed maps in the sense of
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+topological+space#framed_spaces"} framed directed spaces
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#cell_structures Cell structures
          %p
            Closed meshes are regular cell complexes (cf.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CW_complex#Regular_CW_complexes"} Wikipedia entry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stratified+space#fundamental_categories"}
              stratified
              = succeed "-types" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            (meaning the fundamental
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}(M_i,f_i)
            are
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truncated"} truncated
            as
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
          %h3#classification Classification
          %p
            The fundamental categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}(M) = \mathcal{E}(f_n \to f_{n-1} \to ... \to f_0)
            of meshes are certain towers of poset maps ‘with structure’: this gives rise to a combinatorial notion called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trusses"} trusses
            = succeed "-meshes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-trusses" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-truss#combinatorial_meshes"} at trusses
            for details), and
            = succeed "-mesh" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-truss" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-truss#truss_bundle_classifications"} classifying category
          %h3#duality Duality
          %p
            As a consequence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-truss#duality"} truss duality
            and of the classification of meshes by trusses, we obtain a involutive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualization"} dualization
            endofunctor on the category of meshes. This functor can be understood as a geometric dualization operation in the sense of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+duality"} Poincare duality
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#closed_and_open_meshes"}
